---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { href } from '../consts'
import { faqJsonLd } from '../seo'

const columns = ['Flowline', 'RescueTime', 'ActivityWatch', 'Toggl Track', 'ManicTime']

const rows = [
  {
    label: 'Where activity lives',
    cells: [
      'Local SQLite on your PC',
      'Vendor cloud account',
      'Local database',
      'Vendor cloud account',
      'Local by default; commercial product'
    ]
  },
  {
    label: 'Account required',
    cells: ['No', 'Yes', 'No', 'Yes', 'Check their current setup']
  },
  {
    label: 'How time is captured',
    cells: [
      'Automatic foreground window',
      'Automatic',
      'Automatic watchers',
      'Manual timer first',
      'Automatic on Windows'
    ]
  },
  {
    label: 'Browser detail',
    cells: [
      'Domain, with optional extension',
      'Sites, via their client',
      'Browser watchers you install',
      'Depends on the timer entry',
      'App and document tracking'
    ]
  },
  {
    label: 'Price',
    cells: [
      'Free, MIT license',
      'Free tier and paid plans; check current pricing',
      'Free, open source',
      'Free tier and paid plans; check current pricing',
      'Paid commercial app; check current pricing'
    ]
  },
  {
    label: 'Open source',
    cells: ['Yes', 'No', 'Yes', 'No', 'No']
  },
  {
    label: 'Platforms',
    cells: [
      'Windows 10 and 11 only',
      'Windows, Mac, Linux, mobile',
      'Windows, Mac, Linux',
      'Windows, Mac, Linux, mobile, web',
      'Windows, with other editions sold separately'
    ]
  },
  {
    label: 'Team or employer features',
    cells: ['No', 'Yes, on paid plans', 'Not the point of the project', 'Yes', 'Yes, in business editions']
  },
  {
    label: 'Blocks websites',
    cells: ['No', 'No (tracking product)', 'No', 'No', 'No']
  }
]

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Is Flowline a RescueTime alternative?',
    a: 'For personal Windows tracking, yes, if you want automatic capture without a cloud account. RescueTime still fits if you need their reports, coaching, or multi-device sync. Flowline will not replace a team plan.'
  },
  {
    q: 'Is Flowline an ActivityWatch alternative?',
    a: 'Partly. Both store data locally and are open source. Pick ActivityWatch if you want cross-platform watchers and a hackable event log. Pick Flowline if you want a Windows app with rules, a focus dashboard, Pomodoro blocks, and goals already in the UI.'
  },
  {
    q: 'Is Flowline a Toggl Track alternative?',
    a: 'Only if you hate starting a timer. Toggl Track is built around manual time entries and team reporting. Flowline never asks you to press start for ordinary app tracking, and it has no team workspace.'
  },
  {
    q: 'Does Flowline replace a website blocker?',
    a: 'No. It can notify you after a stretch of distracting activity. It does not block sites or apps. Pair it with a blocker if that is the behavior you want.'
  }
]
---

<Base
  title="RescueTime and ActivityWatch Alternative"
  description="Compare Flowline with RescueTime, ActivityWatch, Toggl Track, and ManicTime: local data, automatic tracking, price, and what it does not do."
  jsonLd={faqJsonLd(faqs)}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-content px-5 py-12">
    <div class="max-w-3xl">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-fg">
        Flowline vs RescueTime, ActivityWatch, and other time trackers
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted">
        People usually land here looking for a RescueTime alternative or an ActivityWatch
        alternative that stays on the PC. This table is the practical difference, not a ranking.
        Pricing and plan names change. When a cell depends on the vendor's current offer, it says
        so.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-border">
      <table class="min-w-[52rem] w-full text-left text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-surface/60">
          <tr>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-fg"> </th>
            {columns.map((col) => <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-fg">{col}</th>)}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            rows.map((row) => (
              <tr class="border-t border-border/70">
                <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-fg">{row.label}</th>
                {row.cells.map((cell) => (
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-muted">{cell}</td>
                ))}
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-muted">
      Competitor details describe the products as commonly shipped, not a live scrape of their
      pricing pages. Confirm plans before you buy.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-14 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-fg">RescueTime alternative, with a limit</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
          RescueTime's pitch is automatic tracking plus reports in their cloud. Flowline does the
          automatic part on Windows and stops there: no account, no uploaded timeline, no coaching
          emails. You lose multi-device history and their mobile apps. You keep the SQLite file.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-fg">ActivityWatch alternative, different shape</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
          ActivityWatch is the closest project in spirit. It is local, open source, and more
          portable. You assemble watchers and often live closer to the raw events. Flowline is
          narrower: Windows only, a fixed dashboard, editable category rules, distraction alerts,
          and Pomodoro blocks. If you want to script the data, ActivityWatch is the better base.
          If you want to install and read today, start with Flowline.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
          <a
            href={href('/blog/flowline-vs-activitywatch')}
            class="font-semibold text-primary hover:text-primary-strong"
          >
            Longer note on the two local trackers
          </a>
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-fg">Toggl Track is a timer</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
          Toggl Track expects you to start and stop entries, then report them to a team. Flowline
          never asks for that on ordinary app switches. It is a poor fit if your job is billing
          hours to clients. It is a better fit if you forget the timer exists by 10 a.m.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-fg">ManicTime is the Windows incumbent</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
          ManicTime has tracked Windows apps automatically for years, and it sells business
          editions for teams. Flowline does not compete with those editions. The overlap is
          personal automatic tracking on one PC, with Flowline free and inspectable, and ManicTime
          a paid product with a longer track record. Check their current license before you assume
          a price.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-fg">Questions this comparison gets</h2>
      <div class="mt-6 divide-y divide-border/60">
        {
          faqs.map((faq) => (
            <div class="py-5">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-fg">{faq.q}</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-muted">{faq.a}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="mt-12 rounded-2xl border border-primary/30 bg-surface/40 p-8 text-center">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-fg">Try the Windows build</h2>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-2 max-w-xl text-muted">
        If the table's Flowline column is the one you wanted, install it. If you needed Mac, a
        team dashboard, or a site blocker, it will not grow into those.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
        <a href={href('/download')} class="rounded-xl bg-primary px-6 py-3 font-semibold text-on-primary">
          Download for Windows
        </a>
        <a
          href={href('/faq')}
          class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface/60 px-6 py-3 font-semibold text-fg"
        >
          Read the FAQ
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
